William December Starr (wdstarr@crl.com) wrote:


: The "place" where the posted articles wind up is Usenet, but the
: platforms from which they are launched are usually owned and operated
: by persons other than the posters.  Even where those owners are agents
: of a state or federal government (e.g., a state university), they still
: retain a fair amount of administrative control over the usage of their
: computer accounts.  These administrators, provided that they observe due
: process and other legal requirements, can suspend or revoke people's
: posting privileges.  (And note, of course, that in the case of privately
: owned platforms, what constitutes "due process" is almost completely
: governed by the terms of the provider/customer contract, gmitchell's
: prattlings on about "adhesion clauses" notwithstanding.)

True, every posting on rec.pets.cats gets stored on a system that some
agency pays for and in many cases, its a university (public or private).
Speaking as someone who is a postmaster, I would be hard pressed to find
anything posted to this group that I would consider as justification for
banning someone from using the system I help to run. Note that being a
postmaster does not always mean the same thing as managing a system. This
is true in my case. I am a postmaster, but I report to a person who has
higher management authority on my particular system than I do.

At my university (I am not speaking in an official capacity), I think that
the only way we would be able to justify killing an account for posting
an objectionable message here is if the message contained a threat to the
health or well being of an individual or individuals. Another way I could
justify closing someone's account for network abuse is if the person was
using our facilities for a profit making venture.  

Fortunately, I rarely receive complaints regarding the interactions of my
users with other Usenet readers so this issue of closing accounts down at
my university because of inappropriate Usenet postings has not come up as
far as I know. If someone at my university did clearly threaten the health
or wellbeing of someone else on this group or any other group, I would be
very quick to escalate the matter to my manager, however, if someone sent
me a complaint that someone here likes to have sex with cats (a hypothetical 
example folks, don't get upset), I would not consider that a good reason
to cut that person off from his or her Internet access. I am also not
necessarily speaking for my employer either, but I just don't see what
legal grounds we would have for such punitive measures.

If someone on this group complained to me about a tasteless posting that
one of my users posted here, my reaction would be to simply ask that person
not to post tasteless messages on this group again, but I do not know what
law I could use to force that person to comply unless the person was making
a physical threat of some kind to someone on this group. In general, I find
that people on this group take Usenet much more seriously than they should.
Folks, this is not an academic discussion group and in fact, I could find
much more justification for closing off this entire group to my users than
I could for chastising someone for making tasteless remarks here. Oh, I
guess I should say that I am a frequent reader of this group and I am a
definite cat lover and I do not mean that in any tasteless sense.

