William December Starr (wdstarr@crl.com) wrote:

: Yeah, I probably am.  Note to self: don't try to draft model legislation
: when you've got a Class 3 head cold.  Let's try this:

: If an article (a) is appropriate to the topic of the forum and either
: (b.1) is not presented in an inflammatory manner or (b.2) cannot, even
: with the best of intentions, be presented in anything but an
: inflammatory manner [I'd expect "b.2" cases to be pretty rare, but they
: could happen, I think], then the article is not a violation of the
: charter, even if its posting does result in ignition.

: If an article is not appropriate to the topic of the forum then its
: posting might be a violation of the charter.

: If an article is presented in an avoidably or unnecessarily inflammatory
: manner then its posting might be a violation of the charter.

So how do YOU know an article is unavoidably inflammatory? Again, unless
this grouip is moderated (i.e., the death of this group), no one individual
has the right or authority to decide what is avoidably inflammatory.

: In cases in which a violation of the charter might have occurred, the
: "judges" -- that is, the person or persons whose ruling will carry real
: weight (probably the system administrators at the poster's site) -- will
: use their best judgment to decide whether there was any malice or
: inexcusable negligence involved in the posting of the inappropriate or
: inflammatory article (and, if so, what punishment or action is
: appropriate).  Their primary tools will be common sense, the facts of
: the case at hand, and the poster's prior record, be it good or bad.
Do you think that all system administrators have nothing better to do than
to mediate what are in most cases, petty spats on this group or any other
group on Usenet? Do you also think that all system admins in all countries
have any legal authority to "convict" a user for something said here? 
Please folks, let's talk about reality for a minute. If someone posts a
message here about torturing a cat for fun, do you think most system admins
will have legal grounds for denying Usenet access to someone who is either
paying for the service in good standing through a private service provider
or paying by virtual of being a student at a school where most school admins
abhore censorship and probably would find it extremely difficult to take
any punitive measures for such tasteless postings when there are no hard and
fast LEGAL rules for using Usenet? I think not.

: Additionally, some mechanism of "due process" should be in place to
: ensure that users don't get screwed over by unfair and/or careless
: "judges."  This isn't an issue that I want to delve into right here,
: since it's pretty much independent from the "charter" concept.

Ah, and do you think your average system admin (who may also hate cats),
feels capable of being judge and jury as to what is appropriate or not
on this group? Remember that probably very few system admins read this
group so how do you expect them to know what is and what is not welcome
here? Folks, we are talking about an issue that is much more wide reaching
than the domain of rec.pets.cats. This same issue can be applied to any
discussion group on any medium and it really is a matter in which the
expertise of constitutional lawyers is needed, not just in the United States,
but in any country that has access to this medium. Remember that we are
really discussing a fundamental right to free speech and unless any of you
are a federal judge, I don't think you're in a position to comment on the
legality of censuring (note that I did not say censor) someone for what they
say here. This is a very complicated issue and I do not think that forming
a charter for rec.pets.cats will address this issue because you folks are
trying to solve a problem by fixing the symptom without addressing the 
problem itself. I also do not feel that I am able to adress this issue
from a legal standpoint because I am also not a judge at the federal level.
Also, even at that level, the jurisdiction would not extend to people in
other countries who read this or any other newsgroup.



