anyway -- here isn't "Who owns the newsgroups?" but rather "Who owns the
mechanisms by which people post to the newsgroups?"

Once an article is posted to an unmoderated newsgroup, then yes, it's
entered the realm of net.anarchy and (barring a specific contractual
agreement between the owners/operators of a news-reading site and their
customers), nobody has the right to tamper with it or squelch it.

*But* -- is there any legal or moral requirement that the owners-
/operators of a news-posting site provide their customers with absolute
carte-blanche to post anything they want to any newsgroup they want?
Again, barring a special contractual requirement, I'd say that the
answer is no.  Even on news-posting sites which are owned/operated by
the state and therefore are subject to Constitutional restrictions, some
administrative control over and discipline of the users is permissible.

