In article <2stcq8$orh@rtp.vnet.net>, Lizard@vnet.net (Lizard) said:

>> That's the kicker: when free newspapers are taken/stolen wholesale,
>> something very much akin to force or intimidation _has_ been used, in
>> that the free will of a person who might want to have read the paper
>> has been overridden/negated without his consent.  The confrontative
>> atmosphere of an in-your-face act like blockade picketing is absent,
>> true, but the effective result is the same.  [wdstarr]
>
> Well, this is where I've had trouble. This wasn't a knee-jerk issue
> for me. Is "very much akin to" the same as "is"?
>
> For example, I could legally purchase large quantities of papers (if I
> had the bucks) and achieve the same effects. Assorted protest groups
> will legally buy books, records, whatever, solely to burn them. And so
> on.

Distinction: when an item -- at least, a mass-produced item -- is
purchased, there is a presumption (almost always correct) that the
creator of the product is receiving recompense for is expenditure of
resources.  A corollary of this is that that person now possesses the
resources to repeat the process and place another copy of the item on
the market if he wishes, and a corollary of _that_ is that the party
that wants to keep the item off the market will then have to expend
further resources to purchase the replacement copy, thus providing the
creator with the resources to place yet _another_ copy on the market, et
cetera ad infinitum...

This is clearly not the case when the product costs resources to create,
but is distributed for free.

> There also legitimate reasons to take more than one of something -- I
> could be grabbing a copy for a friend, for example. The sign I
> proposed, which restricting this, is still probably the best choice.

A law against taking most/all copies of a free newspaper for the purpose
of suppressing its dissemination would still allow the taking of
most/all copies for the purposes of assisting its dissemination (e.g.,
somebody grabbing twenty-five or thirty copies from an on-campus
distribution point for the purposes of sticking one in each mailbox at
his off-campus fraternity house).

> If I were to lead a picket against a movie theatre, and the bad press
> caused the theatre owner to change the film to something less
> controversial after a day, I have undoubtedly deprived many people of
> the chance to see the movie, yet I have also acted completely within
> the law and my rights. This might not be a valid example, because the
> movie exists and may still be seen elsewhere. The papers in question
> were destroyed, not merely made slightly harder to find.

In the case of picketing a movie theater, the rights/will of a would-be
audience member have not been negated, since that person only possessed
in the first place the right to view a movie which the owner of the
theater chooses to show in open-to-the-paying-public viewings -- if the
pickets persuade the theater owner not to show the film, the would-be
audience's rights have not been violated.  In the case of free
newspapers, the would-be reader has the right (as I interpret it) to
obtain a copy of and read any newspaper which is freely distributed in
such quantities that should each interested person take one copy, a copy
will remain available to that would-be reader by the time he gets to the
newsstand.  When the implied (or explicitly stated-in-law) "one
interested reader, one copy" rule is violated, the right of the would-be
reader is violated.

> So, as I said, it's hazy for me. It's right on the boundary, to me,
> between legitimate counter-speech (such as writing a letter to a
> sysadmin (g)) and outright censorship (use of force to deprive someone
> of their right of free expression).

I don't see any haze in this example.  Writing a letter to a system
administrator is not an act which can deprive a person of any rights.

-- William December Starr <wdstarr@crl.com>


