> So how do YOU know an article is unavoidably inflammatory? Again,
> unless this grouip is moderated (i.e., the death of this group), no
> one individual has the right or authority to decide what is avoidably
> inflammatory.

False.  A system administrator -- actually, a site owner, but in most
cases the authority will be delegated to a sysadmin -- has the right to
determine whether a posting from an account on that site has violated
that site's Acceptable Use Policies.  If one of the Policies is "Thou
shalt not intentionally violate a newsgroup's charter when posting
there," and if part of a newsgroup's charter is "No inflammatory
articles except for those in which the inflammatory nature is
unavoidable," then the sysadmin does have the right/authority to make
that decision.

> Do you think that all system administrators have nothing better to do
> than to mediate what are in most cases, petty spats on this group or
> any other group on Usenet?

No.

> Do you also think that all system admins in all countries have any
> legal authority to "convict" a user for something said here?

No.

> Please folks, let's talk about reality for a minute. If someone posts
> a message here about torturing a cat for fun, do you think most system
> admins will have legal grounds for denying Usenet access to someone
> who is either paying for the service in good standing through a
> private service provider or paying by virtual of being a student at a
> school where most school admins abhore censorship and probably would
> find it extremely difficult to take any punitive measures for such
> tasteless postings when there are no hard and fast LEGAL rules for
> using Usenet? I think not.

Oddly, I think so.  In the former case, the user is bound by the terms
of the private contract between the user and the provider.  If the user
breaches the contract, the provider can consider itself no longer bound
to deliver on its end of the contract and may cut off service.

In the latter case -- the tuition-paying student -- the administration
still has the authority to enact and enforce rules of usage regarding
academic computer accounts.  The absence of "hard and fast LEGAL rules
for using Usenet" is irrelevant where there _are_ hard and fast rules
for using a computer account which is supplied to one by another party.

> Ah, and do you think your average system admin (who may also hate
> cats), feels capable of being judge and jury as to what is appropriate
> or not on this group? Remember that probably very few system admins
> read this group so how do you expect them to know what is and what is
> not welcome here?

You seem to be hung up on the concept of "Not *all* system administrators
will want to get involved."  Fine -- what I'm working on is a set of
tolls and guidelines which will be available for usage by the admins who
