Date: 7 Jun 1994 16:44:34 GMT
Message-ID: <Mouse.Tue_Jun__7_12_27_07_1994@collatz.mcrcim.mcgill.edu>

[Note the Followup-To:.  The huge list of newsgroups seems almost
entirely inappropriate to me.  If you know of somewhere better suited
to this, feel free to move it there instead.]

In various posts, various people illustrate the great lack of knowledge
of the one-way function used to secure passwords on (almost?) all UNIX
variants.

Specifically, here are some quotes:

>> Nope.  It's not DES.  It's a specially mangled non-reversible algorithm.
> What kind of system are you running!?  [...] man page from a Sun
> specifically states that: [...] IS based on DES algorithm...

>>> I've been laboring for some time under the misapprehension that
>>> DES, especially in the modified form used to encrypt unix
>>> passwords,
>> But, since the UNIX password algorithm isn't DES, [...]
> "UNIX stores a value that is generated by using the password to
> enrypt a block of zeros with a one-way function called crypt(3)...The
> algorithm that crypt(3) is based on is the Data Encryption Standard

First off, these are all somewhat correct.  The algorithm is (usually)
not DES, but it is based on DES.  It is DES with one of the tables
permuted in one of 4096 different ways depending on a salt.  (One of
the 4096 possible salt values produces no change (and thus standard
DES), which is why I said "(usually)" above.)

The quote above is basically the truth: the way the algorithm works is
to take the salt, tweak one of the tables with it (I forget which
table), and then, using the typed password as the key, encrypt
all-zero-bits.  The result is then encrypted again; this continues for
a total of something like 25 encryptions.  The result is rendered
printable by using a base-64 encoding.  The salt is encoded with the
same base-64 encoding and the concatenation of the two is what you find
in /etc/passwd (or /etc/shadow or wherever your hashed passwords are
actually stored).

This last poster I quoted above later posts

> Let me clarify something.  I'm aware of the salt and the other
> modifications that prevent the use of stock DES chips to crack
> passwords.  But since they don't appear to have made any real
> structural changes to the algorithm, what's to stop someone from
> making modified chips specifically to attack crypt(3)?

Nothing.  But making a chip is a relatively expensive operation, not
something your average bored pre-teen cracker on his dad's peecee (to
cite the stereotype) would know how to go about, much less be able to
afford.  Operations that can afford it can generally break in more
cheaply other ways, such as burglary, bribery, or even dumpster-diving.
The human element is still the weakest link in the chain.

If anyone ever does get past the startup cost and make such a chip, and
makes it available relatively cheaply, yes, crypt(3) may fall.  I don't
expect this to happen before general-purpose CPUs get fast enough to
knock off crypt(3) without much trouble.

					der Mouse

			    mouse@collatz.mcrcim.mcgill.edu

