In article <bryner.975.770849625@chemistry.utah.edu>, 
bryner@chemistry.utah.edu (Roger Bryner) said:

>> I guess that we have different opinions, possibly as a result of
>> different childhoods.  Could you please explain why it is "petty,"
>> "malicious," "spiteful," "assholic" (word?) or even "tiresome" to
>> lodge a complaint when one believes that another person has done
>> something which is or may be a wrongful act?  [wdstarr, replying to
>> Kenn Barry]
>
> It is only wrong if your belief of the wrongness of the act itself is
> petty, malicious, spitefull, and assholeish in itself, or if you
> complain to the improper authority, or could have solved it yourself.

Okay.  Taking these three tests in reverse order: 

#3: In my opinion, when dealing with a person who possesses a "bully"
mentality, whether it be a five-year-old on the playground or a
net.vandal of any age, there is no way to solve the problem by oneself
other than by the application of brute force sufficient to impress upon
the bully the fact that he _will_ be hurt if he continues to abuse other
people.  In the playground, this might be achievable via the tactic of
pounding the offender's head into the sand for three or four hours.  On
the net, I don't think it's possible.

#2: I believe that the system administrator at the site from which the
bully is posting is almost always a proper authority to whom to
complain.  Indeed, it's usually the only proper authority.

#1: Is one's belief in the wrongness of the act justified?  Hard to
judge objectively, of course, and subjective viewpoints are useless
(after all, within his own "I can do whatever I want to anybody I want
to do it to" mindset, the bully probably thinks that all of _his_
actions are justified too).  The closest thing to an objective judgment
that I can see is a sort of cumulative ruling of all interested parties,
most notably (but not exclusively) the party to whom the complaint is
made. 

>> Especially when the complainant honestly believes, in light of all
>> the evidence, that the act was committed not out of of ignorance of
>> rules or local custom but rather in a spirit of malice or, at the
>> very least, with intent to be inflammatory and/or just plain
>> unnecessarily rude?
>
> I was not aware that unnecessary rudeness was against the rules.
> Perhaps in your private house, it is, and I would respect such, but in
> public you have no such right to be protected from unnecessary
> rudeness.

No legal right, true (until the rudeness crosses the line into
harassment, anyway), since there aren't any rudeness "rules" which carry
legal weight.  But is it immoral/wrong/whatever to bring to the
attention of a system administrator the fact that user Joe Schmoe is
peppering his public posts from that admin's site with phrases like "Eat
cat shit and die, bitch!"?  In other words, while I don't have a "right"
to be protected from twaddle like this, I believe that I do have the
right to tell someone who _does_ have the right to do something about it
about it.

> Again it comes down to who owns the property.  I think a complaint by
> you means you view yourself as one of the property owners of the thing
> being tresspassed upon.  If this is a group you regularly post on, you
> have merit, but if the other person intends to regularly post also,
> why does he not have the same claim to this "property" that you do?

We both have the same rights... the question is, does that mean that
neither of us has a right to complain to the other's system
administrator, or that both of us have the right to complain to the
other's system administrator?

