Bruce Umbaugh  <BDU100F@ODUVM.BITNET> wrote:
>>At one time, we had a commercial software package, whose license cost was 
>>based on the CPU time burned up in its use.  When folks started coming in 
>>under their friends' passwords [ . . . . ]
>
>>[...] I can't see filing charges for $5 in CPU time...
>
>This is what we've seen when this issue has come up before here.  If
>such matters are treated as theft of service, reasonable calculation
>of damages comes to precious little dinero.  The *monetary* damage
>done is out of proportion to the (perceived?) wrong done.  What's
>your machine worth?  How many cycles does it run in its life?  What
>fraction is the accused said to have pirated?  Won't be much, will it?

Another alternative has occurred to me; in some cases, we may be looking
at a *denial* of service, rather than a *theft* of service.  This line of
thought was prompted by a review of software licensing logs I conducted
this morning.

We have several pieces of software which are license-monitored; in other
words, only X people can run the program at any one time.  If outsiders
are using it, they may prevent legitimate users from doing so.  I'd call
that a 'denial of service.'  Of course, the same problem occurs when a
legitimate user decides to run X copies at the same time...8)  The per-
petrator isn't really *stealing* CPU time or disk space; they are, how-
ever, preventing legitimate users from utilizing those resources.  The
same idea would seem to apply to folks who use University computing re-
sources for personal business, like the person I discovered running a
for-profit typing service for the general public out of our PC labs.  

>THe perception of the wrong being so great
>leads folks to do goofy things like estimate that a document is worth
>tens of thousands of dollars (E911).  Silliness abounds--to say nothing
>of real wrongs to real people.  

Unfortunately, you are correct.  It would seem that more bureaucrats
are interested in the stick than are interested in the carrot.  

>One doctrine . . . ooooh, I hesitate to raise this . . . that might
>make sense to evaluate damages in "theft of service" cases is to look
>at the damages that *might reasonably have been foreseen* to result, or
>that *might have resulted*.  Codes of conduct can warrant holding
>students liable for severe infractions for throwing heavy objects from
>the dorm window *even if no one is crushed thereby*.  (I think the Model
>Penal Code addresses this.)

I don't really see how we can make such anticipations in the online
world.  Heck, a person running the lowly /bin/sh can suck up enough 
resources to thrash (or crash) the system without running any other 
program!

>Another possibility is to find a category other than theft of service
>under which to pursue these computer violations.

We're reaching for a Catch-22 right about here...some of the more moderate
admins (like me) would rather see a generic "misuse/abuse of system" or
"unauthorized system access" category for such violations, but many folks
reject such categories as excessively broad, excessively vague, or both.

>>Of course, this is all moot if no money is changing hands;
>>I can't see a non-chargeback computing center claiming 'theft' if *no one*
>>pays for access/use.  In those cases, I'd think that 'misuse of services'
>>is the worst one could claim.
>
>Hmmm, maybe.  I think that there have been successful prosecutions for
>"stealing" large quantities of free newspapers.  

It seems to me that, while the letter of the law addresses physical
theft, the *intent* is to prevent the *denial of access to others*.
If this is true, it would seem to reinforce the 'denial of service'
approach to online cases such as those we're discussing.

>Always a pleasure!

Indeed.  At the risk of blowing a Good Thing, I'll mention that several
new users have told me (via email) that the alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
newsgroup is being used as an example of "Usenet quality" by the Inter-
net/Usenet trainers at their universities.  I'd like to offer my per-
sonal appreciation to the participants here as well...

--Wes

