>]>>a school library based upon some content-based restriction on that
>]>>material, then principally there is no difference.

>]>>steve nichols

>]>Steve -- the difference is big no matter how you slice it -- unless you
>]>are saying that each is in some way a restriction in what people are
>]>allowed to read (rather like saying that all people/mammals/living things
>]>are essencially the same since they all have opposable thumbs/warm
>]>blood/DNA).  The difference here is similar to the difference between
>]>shooting a rabbit (or a possum in Australia) and spotted owl.  There are
>]>lots of the former, so shooting one does not endanger the species, while
>]>shooting the latter does.  

>]You may be addressing the difference in effect, but not in principle.  
>]Actively removing materials from a public library because one dislikes
>]their content is principally censorship, regardless of the availability
>]of such materials elsewhere.  No one has a right to remove materials 
>]from a library based solely on the assumption that such materials are
>]available elsewhere.

>You're missing the big difference.  Taking all the papers is stealing them,
>while the library custodian pulling the book off the shelf is (ostensibly)
>within his authority.                          

>Stealing the newspapers can be equated with an outside party stealing a 
>book, not just from one library, but from all libraries in the world, if
>those thieving students were stealing the papers from all their distribution
>points.

I have no quarrel with the idea that one is worse than the other.

The question I am addressing is whether either is ethical.  My point
is that neither is ethical.  The fact that one does more harm than the
other does not by itself do anything to show that one is not censorship.

>Here's my view:  it's worse when thugs steal papers or steal books, because
>unlike institutional censorship, there's nobody to protest against. 

steve nichols

