n9348795@gonzo.cc.wwu.edu (Blain Nelson) writes:

>nichols@ux1.cso.uiuc.edu (Steven Nichols) writes:


>>You may be addressing the difference in effect, but not in principle.  
>>Actively removing materials from a public library because one dislikes
>>their content is principally censorship, regardless of the availability
>>of such materials elsewhere.  No one has a right to remove materials 
>>from a library based solely on the assumption that such materials are
>>available elsewhere.

>No one has a right to steal materials from a library on any assumption for
>any reason.  The people who run the library can and do choose not to
>acquire books for whatever reasons they choose.  They also can and do
>choose to remove books from their shelves for whatever reasons they
>choose.  Those who run a library must be free to make these choices on
>their own -- no matter how poorly they are made.  Interfering with their
>right to choose not to own a book would be -- I propose -- far greater
>censorship than the choice not to own a book.

A public library is a public trust, not the property of those to 
whom the responsibility is given to maintain it.  Therefore, we may
make judgements about how responsibly our librarians maintain our
libraries by certain governing ethical principles.  It would be a mistake
to assume that the administrator of a public service has a right to 
excercise whimsical decisions not related to the well-being of that 
public service.

steve nichols


