Kadie) writes:
> 
> > p.s. I also dislike the idea of unmoderated newsgroups that restrict
> > viewpoint.
> 
> I really don't understand why, to be honest.
> 
> I can understand the position that all newsgroups ought to be open to
> all viewpoints.  I can also understand the position that newsgroups
> ought to be restricted by content, with the assumption that content
> that's unwelcome in one newsgroup will always be welcome in some other
> newsgroup.  (Note that this second position is intellectually honest
> only if there really is enough diversity in newsgroups so that all
> material can be posted to some newsgroup or another.  At present I
> think that's true, subject to the usual caveats about copyrights,
> patents, trademarks, libel, national security, obscenity laws, and
> other forms of censorship that are widely accepted in most countries.)
> 
> What I don't understand, though, is acting as if one particular
> mechanism for content restriction is OK, while other aren't.  This
> strikes me as a technical detail more than anything else.  I just
> don't understand why it's a matter of principle.
> 

A moderated newsgroup is analogous to a private forum.  The only messages 
posted to the group meet the editorial approval of the moderator.

An unmoderated newsgroup is analogous to a discussion on a street corner.  
Anyone who comes along is free to say what they like.  They are also free to 
try to introduce a new topic for discussion (i.e., a new thread).  Anyone 
present who is uncomfortable with any of the discussions taking place on the 
street corner is free to go elsewhere for a private discussion (i.e., a 
moderated newsgroup, E-mail, etc.).

I agree with Carl.  I believe that unmoderated newsgroups should remain free 
and open.  The appropriate place to enforce charters is in moderated 
newsgroups.

It is important to keep in mind that the proponents of charter enforcement, 
etc., are generally not trying to suppress discussions that have absolutely no 
logical relationship to the newsgroup.  Take the current controversy in 
rec.pets.cats as an example. A young kid posted some humorous messages on cats. 
 Some of the members in rec.pets.cats were offended by those stories.  So, they 
yell that his stories were off-topic and try to have his network access revoked 
-- successfully.  Rec.pets.cats is a forum for the discussion of cats.  It is 
not just limited to "substantive" postings.  "Cute" stories are posted and 
welcomed by these same individuals.  This young man was not posting stories 
about Chevrolets, shortwave radio, etc.

Something similar happened to me in rec.pets.cats.  When I heard about what 
happened to young man, I started this very thread in rec.pets.cats.  I wanted 
to call to the attention of that group how someone had been mistreated by that 
same particular group.  These same individuals again yelled off-topic and tried 
to have my network access revoked -- unsuccessfully.  I was not discussing 
censorship in general when I started this thread.  I was discussing censorship 
by that very group.  I was not discussing censorship in alt.fan.barney there.

The question of relevance is a red herring argument.  I am willing to bet that 
if someone posted a message on Chevrolets in rec.pets.cats, no one would 
object, because no one would feel threatened and therefore no one would bother 
to read it.

I have not seen a time when the question of relevance and charters has come up 
when those raising the question were not feeling hostile and threatened.  I 
have not seen those questions raised as a public issue where the messages 
objected to were logically unrelated in any way to the group in question.

Charters and accusations of irrelevance are the cries of the intolerant.

