nichols@ux1.cso.uiuc.edu (Steven Nichols) writes:

>n9348795@gonzo.cc.wwu.edu (Blain Nelson) writes:

>A public library is a public trust, not the property of those to 
>whom the responsibility is given to maintain it.  Therefore, we may
>make judgements about how responsibly our librarians maintain our
>libraries by certain governing ethical principles.  It would be a mistake
>to assume that the administrator of a public service has a right to 
>excercise whimsical decisions not related to the well-being of that 
>public service.

This is of course true and, consequently, irrelevant to my point.  Because
the nature of that public trust has to be defined in a functional way for
librarians to be able to do the job they have to do, although it is
impossible to define that in an absolute way.  As importantly, the public
is a large group with a broad spectrum of feelings, opinions, and values. 
Trying to best respond to that with human librarians who have a right to
their own feelings, opinions, and values is going to be a sporadic process
(which is not to imply that this is a whimsical business, or that any
librarian treats it as such) no matter what.  To have to make the
decisions that have to be made on what to acquire and what to get rid of
is a tricky process anyway -- one made more difficult by the fear that the
decision to not have any given book on the shelf will bring cries of
censorship which may not abate no matter how good the reasoning for that
decision might be.  

I suppose we could require every library to hold every book ever
published, but besides being impossible, it would be silly to do so.  None
of which changes the fact that there is an elemental difference betwûeen
having a book removed from a library and stealing every copy of a free
newspaper.  This difference is obvious unless you take an all-or-nothing
approach to the issue of censorship.  I would propose that "censorship"
which is unorganized, localized, and is functionally indistiguishable from
use of reasonable discretion (and which does not jeopardize the
availability of a product to those truly interested in acquiring it) is
not worth spending time worrying about and may not even be censorship anyway.

Have fun with that.

Blain
